10
00:00:42,230 --> 00:00:51,670
Dave, you're the direct rendering manager subsystem maintainer in the Linux kernel and you have nearly two decades of kernel maintenance experience and

11
00:00:51,670 --> 00:00:55,551
I'm super happy to learn from you throughout the episode. Are you ready to dive in?

12
00:00:55,560 --> 00:00:59,150
Yes, let's go. Okay.

13
00:00:59,150 --> 00:01:03,710
So, at the very start, I mentioned a moment ago that you're the maintainer of something that's called DRM.

14
00:01:03,710 --> 00:01:08,990
Can you just tell us quickly what is DRM so we know the kind of work you do on the Linux kernel?

15
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,950
Well, since DRM is a bit of an overloaded term, it's not the bad one. I suppose is the best way to describe it.

16
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:22,590
It's a thing called the direct rendering manager, which is a legacy name for what was really just the GPU or graphics support subsystem in the Linux kernel.

17
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:29,030
The name was given to a small component, maybe 20 years ago and we have expanded on it, but we just kept the name.

18
00:01:29,030 --> 00:01:30,790
So, I think the name is DRM.

19
00:01:30,790 --> 00:01:33,670
I don't think direct rendering manager even really makes sense for what it does anymore.

20
00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:40,830
So, stick with the acronym, but it pretty much when you hear DRM, just think GPU graphics accelerators.

21
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:43,070
Okay.

22
00:01:43,070 --> 00:01:48,510
So, we're going to use DRM and your experience with maintaining it throughout the episode, but to get us started,

23
00:01:48,510 --> 00:01:56,710
we want to talk a little bit about some of the scale and structure issues associated with the Linux kernel and we're going to talk about subsystem workflows and

24
00:01:56,710 --> 00:02:03,430
how you do release engineering and we'll use DRM as concrete examples throughout the episode.

25
00:02:03,430 --> 00:02:09,389
So with that in mind, can you tell us a little bit about the scope and the architecture of the Linux kernel?

26
00:02:09,399 --> 00:02:11,670
How many maintainers are there?

27
00:02:11,670 --> 00:02:13,910
How many subsystems are there?

28
00:02:13,910 --> 00:02:15,790
Please give us a few initial insights.

29
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:22,591
Yeah, the Linux kernel is well, we consider it's probably the biggest software engineering project in the world. That's what we like to say.

30
00:02:22,591 --> 00:02:29,590
In terms of maintainers, the number is very changeable, I would say on average it's between 80 and 100 and 150.

31
00:02:29,590 --> 00:02:33,910
The varying levels of what a maintainer is and where they sit in the hierarchy moves a lot.

32
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:39,350
We have every year maintainer summit where Linus invites like the top 30 maintainers to that.

33
00:02:39,350 --> 00:02:48,750
So that's kind of a number of like the top level people that Linus needs to talk to, but that scales out there into a hierarchy that can expand to up to 100, I would say, in the kernel at the moment.

34
00:02:48,750 --> 00:02:54,070
In terms of subsystems, again, there's no strict definition of what exactly a subsystem is.

35
00:02:54,070 --> 00:02:55,990
A subsystem is something that a maintainer maintains usually.

36
00:02:55,990 --> 00:03:03,070
So in the same sense of there is the number of subsystems that there are the number of maintainers and some maybe not one is to one, but it's pretty close.

37
00:03:03,070 --> 00:03:09,790
But there are a number of major subsystems would probably be like the graphics subsystem, the networking subsystem,

38
00:03:09,790 --> 00:03:18,430
the CPU like x86 support, arm64 support and those are the major areas, storage, file systems, memory management.

39
00:03:18,430 --> 00:03:20,470
So they're probably the biggest groupings that we have.

40
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:28,270
Okay, so we've got DRM as one subsystem and CPU scheduling or memory management or file systems.

41
00:03:28,270 --> 00:03:31,110
Those are all examples of subsystems.

42
00:03:31,110 --> 00:03:37,790
So if we take DRM as an example, how many people are actually working with you as the maintainer of the DRM subsystem?

43
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:50,830
If you'd asked me this yesterday, I would have said 100, but I actually ran the numbers last night and apparently we have over 300 people contributing every kernel release to the DRM subsystem, which actually surprised me just as much.

44
00:03:50,830 --> 00:03:52,510
So they don't all work directly with me again.

45
00:03:52,510 --> 00:03:55,670
We have a hierarchical system.

46
00:03:55,670 --> 00:04:01,270
I probably work on a weekly to fortnightly basis with maybe 20 people in that range, 15 to 20.

47
00:04:01,270 --> 00:04:04,750
And then that they work in the hierarchy with the other grouping.

48
00:04:04,750 --> 00:04:08,510
But yes, I was surprised that we have up to 300 contributors per kernel for the DRM subsystem.

49
00:04:08,520 --> 00:04:17,870
Okay, so there's 300 people and they're somehow reporting to you and you're managing their work and you're reviewing their pull requests.

50
00:04:17,870 --> 00:04:27,670
Can you give us at a high-level a rough idea, what are those 300 people doing and then what are you doing when it comes to managing the infrastructure for DRM in the Linux kernel?

51
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:36,310
So this is probably an area where the DRM subsystem is a little bit different to a lot of the other subsystems in that I actually have quite a large hierarchy.

52
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:42,710
I would think more like a corporate style of There is two co-maintainers, myself and Simon Vetter.

53
00:04:42,710 --> 00:04:51,350
And then below us we have a range of maybe six or seven more feeding into us and then they have a range of people feeding into them and then there's all of the front-line developers probably feeding into that.

54
00:04:51,360 --> 00:05:00,830
So the hierarchy is, depending where you sit on it, just like in a corporate structure, is very different for what your job is.

55
00:05:00,830 --> 00:05:08,390
So my role, a lot of it is I suppose I would call facilitating and creating an environment for people to be able to work in and be happy to work in.

56
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:13,190
My main weekly task is I deal with Linus.

57
00:05:13,190 --> 00:05:15,430
I submit the pull request to Linus on a weekly basis.

58
00:05:15,430 --> 00:05:18,590
I gather all the pull requests from the people below me and I send them up to Linus.

59
00:05:18,590 --> 00:05:27,390
The amount of review I personally do on those pull requests is very limited at this point because they are so large already that

60
00:05:27,390 --> 00:05:33,830
I have to trust that the maintainers below me have done appropriate review and they have to trust that the maintainers below them have done appropriate review.

61
00:05:33,830 --> 00:05:43,830
The review all happens in public on the mailing list so we can always go back and reference it, but for me personally on a daily weekly basis, I don't often review the pull requests that that much.

62
00:05:43,830 --> 00:05:45,909
I do a set of standard builds on them.

63
00:05:45,909 --> 00:05:49,391
I make sure there's no they don't build. That's a big problem.

64
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:54,910
But mostly it's a maintenance role for me is very different than a standard maintainer.

65
00:05:54,910 --> 00:06:02,790
A lot of the standard maintainers will be applying the patches to our trees, checking the quality of the work, and making sure that it's suitable for going upstream to me.

66
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:04,670
Thanks, that's really helpful.

67
00:06:04,670 --> 00:06:15,790
So, I have the understanding that people are in a way working for or reporting to you, and you mentioned a moment ago that you're reporting to Linus Torvalds as well.

68
00:06:15,790 --> 00:06:22,710
Can you tell us a little bit more about how you report to Linus and what type of work you do on behalf of this core leader for the Linux kernel?

69
00:06:22,720 --> 00:06:27,110
Yeah, so one thing with the kernel is Linus is very delicate.

70
00:06:27,110 --> 00:06:34,630
He delegates a lot of trust to the top-level maintainers, and it is kind of like I hate the word fiefdom, but you own your garden.

71
00:06:34,630 --> 00:06:40,590
Like you're in charge of maintaining that section of the kernel, and he trusts you until you give him a reason not to, I suppose.

72
00:06:40,590 --> 00:06:42,350
I've worked with him now for 20 years.

73
00:06:42,350 --> 00:06:44,310
I've had a few public falling out with him.

74
00:06:44,310 --> 00:06:45,909
They're easy to find on the internet.

75
00:06:45,909 --> 00:06:49,390
But overall, we don't have a huge amount of interaction.

76
00:06:49,390 --> 00:06:53,230
Most of my interactions with Linus are at the maintainer summit once a year.

77
00:06:53,230 --> 00:06:56,750
We'll meet up and we'll have it We'll just talk about life, but mostly it's about normal things.

78
00:06:56,750 --> 00:06:59,950
We both like scuba diving, so there's a cool you know, things like that.

79
00:06:59,950 --> 00:07:09,909
But in terms of the kernel, every week I send him an official pull request with the list of all of the fixes for that week from all of the people in my group.

80
00:07:09,909 --> 00:07:12,909
And he then will process that.

81
00:07:12,909 --> 00:07:18,030
If he processes that, the system will send an automatic reply telling me that Linus has taken the request.

82
00:07:18,030 --> 00:07:21,830
If you get a reply from Linus himself, that's usually only if you've done something wrong.

83
00:07:21,830 --> 00:07:23,830
He is not really good at giving positive feedback.

84
00:07:23,830 --> 00:07:27,909
He is much better at giving something wrong feedback.

85
00:07:27,909 --> 00:07:30,110
He's known for not giving the positive side.

86
00:07:30,110 --> 00:07:36,470
So, you generally look for the Linus on the reply and hope it you don't see it, and then things go along swimmingly.

87
00:07:36,470 --> 00:07:46,110
Once every Like we'll go into the release cycle later, but once every 9 or 10 weeks you have to send the big pull request, which is all of the work that is due for the next kernel.

88
00:07:46,110 --> 00:07:56,470
That's the one that often upsets him because that's the one where you will have a lot of change, and there is always regression with change, and you just have to hope that he doesn't catch a really bad regression, or you haven't messed up some other part of the kernel.

89
00:07:56,480 --> 00:08:01,990
But, in general, my interactions with Linus are purely on the I send him a weekly email.

90
00:08:01,990 --> 00:08:03,590
I hope he doesn't have a problem with it.

91
00:08:03,590 --> 00:08:10,270
If he has a problem with it, I delegate that down to the people who caused the problem, and I generally accept the I'm an umbrella, I suppose, as well.

92
00:08:10,270 --> 00:08:19,150
You know, need to protect the people under me from his wrath, and he's calmed down over the years, but I'm used to it at this stage, so it's I sometimes have to take an hour or two to breathe, and then I'm good.

93
00:08:19,160 --> 00:08:29,750
Okay, so that's really giving us a nice sense in the day of the life of a Linux kernel maintainer, and we're going to do a deep dive into all those topics in a moment.

94
00:08:29,750 --> 00:08:37,390
But, before we do that, can you tell us a little bit about some of the specific challenges that you face when it comes to maintaining DRM in the Linux kernel?

95
00:08:37,400 --> 00:08:45,270
I think the biggest challenge I've faced over the years, and Simon, and my co-maintainer, has been great at helping with this,

96
00:08:45,270 --> 00:08:53,550
is that the scale of things when you get to being this big is quite hard to both communicate to other maintainers,

97
00:08:53,550 --> 00:08:59,790
and to have interesting topics with other co-maintainers who are dealing at the 10 people level, or the 40 people level.

98
00:08:59,790 --> 00:09:04,190
They don't quite understand why the scale of changes when you get to our level.

99
00:09:04,190 --> 00:09:07,270
Like, even when I was taking with 100 people going through it, the scale problem is different.

100
00:09:07,270 --> 00:09:14,550
But, now that I'm taking 300, I'm like, "Okay, now our scale problems are definitely valid and different than theirs."

101
00:09:14,550 --> 00:09:17,110
So, for example, we have to have a hierarchy.

102
00:09:17,110 --> 00:09:25,150
We have a common tree where everyone has commit access for a bunch of things, and we try to have a group maintainership model, which isn't something as standard in the kernel.

103
00:09:25,150 --> 00:09:30,990
X86 subsystem kind of did it with three people. We've done it now with maybe 30 to 40 people. I think in our group maintainers, there's probably more.

104
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:36,950
There's three maintainers, but there's a group of committers. So, that system is different than everyone else's. That's probably one of the biggest challenges.

105
00:09:36,960 --> 00:09:44,790
Like, scaling the standard kernel development processes up to our size of the subsystem has been the biggest challenge we faced over the last years.

106
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:56,630
And in my world, a lot of my job has been to just sort of step away and not let my, I suppose, ego or my own niched workflows drive everything else's.

107
00:09:56,630 --> 00:10:03,990
And I need to accept that, "Okay, this is something that makes the community better, and maybe it makes my life a bit harder,

108
00:10:03,990 --> 00:10:09,790
but I need to use these scripts or just develop methods technology because it makes it easier for everyone else.

109
00:10:09,790 --> 00:10:15,150
And often we have a problem where over the years we got our little niche of how we develop things and we stay in it.

110
00:10:15,150 --> 00:10:20,510
And being able to not do that has been very good for me and in the helping the subsystem to grow, I think, because scale is hard.

111
00:10:20,520 --> 00:10:30,550
So, you've talked about scale from the perspective of the number of humans, and you mentioned there are like 300 people who are developing various types of patches or change sets.

112
00:10:30,550 --> 00:10:43,310
Can you tell our listeners a little bit about the size and the scope and the scale of your part of the Linux kernel when it comes to things like lines of code or size of a change set? Other details that you can share there?

113
00:10:43,320 --> 00:10:53,550
Yeah, the lines of code is a bit of an outlier for us because we have a huge amount of auto-generated headers, which both piss people off and are very valuable for us for a few drivers.

114
00:10:53,550 --> 00:10:59,030
But, I think we're talking in the at least a million lines of code under my purview in that subsystem.

115
00:10:59,030 --> 00:11:02,230
The headers are probably add another million or half a million onto that.

116
00:11:02,230 --> 00:11:06,110
But, so yeah, you're probably talking at least in the 1 million to 2 million lines of code.

117
00:11:06,110 --> 00:11:15,150
I don't have accurate numbers on the number of change sets I process, but I would say it's in the 2,000 to 3,000 every kernel cycle.

118
00:11:15,150 --> 00:11:17,390
So, every 3 months I'd say we put in about 2 to 3,000.

119
00:11:17,390 --> 00:11:24,550
Of that, I would say I probably process maybe 50 to 60 pull requests from sub-maintainers in that cycle as well.

120
00:11:24,560 --> 00:11:36,190
On average on a week, I'd say I process maybe five to six pull requests from sub-maintainers, and they normally at this stage of the fixes stages of the kernel, probably only have 10 to 15 patches in them.

121
00:11:36,190 --> 00:11:40,110
But everything in the big part of the merge request, that's where all the majority of things come through.

122
00:11:40,120 --> 00:11:49,030
That makes a lot of sense. Now, I know many people who use Linux, they have heard of this idea of an RC build or a release candidate build.

123
00:11:49,030 --> 00:12:00,350
And so in this next phase of our show, I'm hoping that we can give the listeners a sense of your workflow or the cadence or the processes that you follow in Linux kernel maintenance.

124
00:12:00,350 --> 00:12:06,870
So, can you tell us a little bit about how many weeks you work for until you get to a release candidate?

125
00:12:06,870 --> 00:12:09,150
Can you give us a few details about that process as we dive in?

126
00:12:09,160 --> 00:12:13,870
Yeah, I'll probably give the general overview of how the kernel works and probably a bit more into what we do.

127
00:12:13,870 --> 00:12:20,350
So, overall in the kernel has what we call a 9-to-10 week release cycle, depending on how happy Linus is.

128
00:12:20,350 --> 00:12:24,070
It can go 9 weeks, it can go 10 weeks if he feels there's been a reason.

129
00:12:24,070 --> 00:12:25,950
Mostly, we've been doing 9 weeks lately.

130
00:12:25,950 --> 00:12:28,150
We had one 10 week last time.

131
00:12:28,150 --> 00:12:32,030
The way that works is the first 2 weeks of every new kernel development cycle are called the merge window.

132
00:12:32,040 --> 00:12:42,910
That's where Linus takes all of the pull requests from all of the authors, all of the maintainers under him, and he puts them all together and stabilizes that for 2 weeks.

133
00:12:42,910 --> 00:12:44,710
And we're hoping in that 2 weeks that that's a runnable kernel.

134
00:12:44,710 --> 00:12:48,350
At the end of that 2 weeks, he'll release what's called RC1.

135
00:12:48,350 --> 00:12:56,710
And then on a weekly basis, usually on a Sunday afternoon Pacific time, he will release RC2, RC3 up until RC7 usually.

136
00:12:56,710 --> 00:13:00,790
And then from RC7, he'll release the final one the following week. Occasionally, it'll be an RC8.

137
00:13:00,800 --> 00:13:02,750
Maybe there's been an RC9 once.

138
00:13:02,750 --> 00:13:04,350
But it goes through that cop that cycle.

139
00:13:04,350 --> 00:13:08,671
Every week after the merge window is just fixes. And everything should be stable.

140
00:13:08,680 --> 00:13:12,110
There should be no new regressions. Everything There should be no new code.

141
00:13:12,120 --> 00:13:15,550
Linus comes down very heavily if you try to sneak things in during those weeks.

142
00:13:15,560 --> 00:13:25,710
He's he's very specific that the merge window is where you drop new stuff and all after that should just be fixes for It technically should just be fixes for regressions.

143
00:13:25,710 --> 00:13:30,350
But we often add like to fixes for general things that we need to back port or we need to stabilize.

144
00:13:30,350 --> 00:13:33,630
But, in saying that, a lot of people have a misconception about the merge window.

145
00:13:33,640 --> 00:13:39,870
The merge window is for Linus to merge the trees that other people have prepared prior to the merge window.

146
00:13:39,870 --> 00:13:43,030
The merge window is not when you should be preparing your tree for Linus.

147
00:13:43,030 --> 00:13:46,070
The merge window is not when you should be doing development that needs to go to Linus.

148
00:13:46,080 --> 00:14:00,110
All that stuff should generally have been ready for Linus prior to the merge window and it will have been in a tree we call Linux next where somebody will gather all those disparate trees across the internet and just does merges every day.

149
00:14:00,110 --> 00:14:10,150
That's their job just to sit there merging every day and find all the conflicting problems and make sure we understand that building the tree is hard and that merging all these conflicts will happen and that we know about them in advance.

150
00:14:10,150 --> 00:14:18,030
So, when Linus gets to do all that final merges into his tree, he has a history of all of the conflicts and he has knows what's going to cause problems.

151
00:14:18,030 --> 00:14:28,430
He will accept things that haven't been in Linux next, but he will be often very unhappy about that or he will sometimes just say no. It's generally preferred that we have stuff ready in advance.

152
00:14:28,430 --> 00:14:30,470
Again, that's very subsystem dependent on how that happens.

153
00:14:30,480 --> 00:14:34,910
I'll go into how we do it in the graphics subsystem.

154
00:14:34,910 --> 00:14:37,430
We have a hierarchy of trees that come from vendors.

155
00:14:37,430 --> 00:14:42,670
So, we like have an AMD GPU tree, a tree from Intel, we have tree for Qualcomm.

156
00:14:42,670 --> 00:14:51,550
And then we also have a misc tree for all of the smaller drivers will cohabitate and they will all push their changes to that.

157
00:14:51,550 --> 00:14:53,950
We have a hierarchy called a next tree that we have open all the time.

158
00:14:53,950 --> 00:14:58,630
And when that happen, Linus releases RC6, we will generally shut our next tree down.

159
00:14:58,640 --> 00:15:07,310
If your stuff isn't in our next tree by RC6, which gives us usually a two-week window for us to stabilize, we will leave it for the next kernel cycle.

160
00:15:07,310 --> 00:15:11,750
You can push it into the next tree, but it won't get merged till the next kernel cycle.

161
00:15:11,750 --> 00:15:13,550
And that's how we've done that over the years.

162
00:15:13,550 --> 00:15:20,870
We initially were a lot worse at that, but we've stabilized in the last four or five years onto that system and it seems to be working quite well for us.

163
00:15:20,870 --> 00:15:23,110
Pushing in stuff after RC6 is at my discretion usually.

164
00:15:23,120 --> 00:15:36,790
Uh and occasionally I will do it if it's something I'm personally interested in or it's sort of a piece of hardware that we need to get working, but we try to maintain that because we found for stability that allowing a free-for-all after RC6 it hasn't led to quality results, put it that way.

165
00:15:36,790 --> 00:15:43,910
So yes, so generally that's how we work is that we set everything up in advance by RC6, Linus opens his tree two weeks later,

166
00:15:43,910 --> 00:15:50,510
I will send the big huge pull request of everything that was there at the previous RC6 to Linus, he will accept that, he will pull everyone else's in.

167
00:15:50,520 --> 00:16:02,830
When he hits RC1, we will pull that into our tree and then start generating the fixes trees based on that and then start the next bring all the work that happened between RC6 and that point into a next tree and go again for the next window.

168
00:16:02,840 --> 00:16:11,030
Okay, so that's really helpful and you're giving us some insights into release candidates and how they're actually built and the cycle that you go through.

169
00:16:11,030 --> 00:16:17,550
Just to make sure all of our listeners are on the same page, you mentioned the words regression and then also the word tree.

170
00:16:17,550 --> 00:16:25,190
Can you concretely define a regression and the tree and give us some other insights into how those work in kernel maintenance?

171
00:16:25,200 --> 00:16:28,550
I'll start with tree because it's probably easier.

172
00:16:28,550 --> 00:16:36,550
Now is a git tree is what we call the kernel tree is really just the kernel checkout, but now with git it's the git tree, the git checkout, the git repos.

173
00:16:36,550 --> 00:16:44,110
So we have like a number of git repositories that we use that are we then have some for DRM, we've got a multiple of them for DRM and then there's what we build a hierarchy from.

174
00:16:44,120 --> 00:16:51,950
So when I say tree that's it, it's just those git flows of tops into Linus to me.

175
00:16:51,950 --> 00:16:58,790
And for regression, well, I'd love to define it properly, but yeah, a regression is made to be something that broke in RC1.

176
00:16:58,790 --> 00:17:06,110
If something is merged during RC1 that causes anything to go be worse than it was before RC1, then it's considered a regression.

177
00:17:06,110 --> 00:17:16,910
The standard method of dealing with regressions is to revert first, ask questions later, so we should remove the patch that caused the problem and then work out what the problem was and fix it in the next cycle.

178
00:17:16,910 --> 00:17:20,590
That doesn't always happen, engineers are not brilliant at dealing with regressions in that way.

179
00:17:20,590 --> 00:17:25,829
Often their instinct is to try and fix it first, and that often causes a lot of discussion on what is a regression.

180
00:17:25,829 --> 00:17:27,550
Is this considered a regression?

181
00:17:27,550 --> 00:17:32,310
If it's a regression two kernels ago, is it something that should be fixed urgently now?

182
00:17:32,310 --> 00:17:34,230
And sometimes it should be more urgent.

183
00:17:34,230 --> 00:17:41,830
So, there's a lot of scope for what actually regression is, but Linus's technical definition has always been something that broke in RC1 and made life worse for a user.

184
00:17:41,840 --> 00:17:43,910
Okay, that's really helpful.

185
00:17:43,910 --> 00:17:48,270
So, is a regression something that's connected to performance or correctness?

186
00:17:48,270 --> 00:17:50,550
I'm guessing that it could be both of those. Is that right?

187
00:17:50,560 --> 00:17:52,231
Yes, indeed. Anything, to be honest.

188
00:17:52,231 --> 00:17:56,070
His definition is if it made something worse for a user.

189
00:17:56,070 --> 00:18:02,870
Whether that user is having a performance difference, or that user is seeing a functional break in their hardware.

190
00:18:02,870 --> 00:18:06,230
But, his thing is if nobody notices, it's the old if the old tree falls in the wood.

191
00:18:06,230 --> 00:18:11,910
If a regression happens and nobody notices, it's not considered a regression till someone notices.

192
00:18:11,910 --> 00:18:14,870
We don't urgently find them, but yeah, we generally have to be a user driving them.

193
00:18:14,880 --> 00:18:23,150
Cool. So, in the context of the graphics subsystem and accelerators, can you give us a few concrete examples of what a regression would look like?

194
00:18:23,160 --> 00:18:29,110
Quite often, a regression actually looks like your laptop screen not turning on for us.

195
00:18:29,110 --> 00:18:36,310
That's the easiest one to give is like, my laptop screen is now flickering or not doing what it used to do when I had the last kernel and I got the new kernel.

196
00:18:36,310 --> 00:18:40,510
Often it's some of the rendering on the screen might have an issue.

197
00:18:40,510 --> 00:18:42,550
There maybe my hot plug stopped working.

198
00:18:42,550 --> 00:18:44,190
My suspend and resume stopped working.

199
00:18:44,190 --> 00:18:51,990
Like in the old days, the more common ones were like, yeah, suspend resume stopped working, or your laptop just doesn't light up anymore, which are fairly obvious regressions.

200
00:18:51,990 --> 00:18:56,990
In other cases, more like on in the data center and accelerators phase, it's like, yeah, our workload has gotten slower.

201
00:18:56,990 --> 00:18:58,830
Like, I have this standard workload I test for.

202
00:18:58,840 --> 00:19:01,990
I've upgraded to a new kernel, and suddenly it's not working as well.

203
00:19:01,990 --> 00:19:12,630
Or, say you have some device like a Steam Deck and you're trying to move from an older kernel to a newer kernel, and all of a sudden the newer kernel device isn't cooling as well, or it's not scheduling as well.

204
00:19:12,630 --> 00:19:14,630
You're not seeing the same frame numbers you were.

205
00:19:14,630 --> 00:19:20,630
Those are all considered regressions, and if you are when reported the generally will be tried to be hunted down and fixed.

206
00:19:20,640 --> 00:19:30,630
And normally those regressions are reported to you and your team by users, that is people who are actually using the Linux kernel on your devices. Is that the right way to think about it?

207
00:19:30,640 --> 00:19:34,470
Yeah, often there will be engineers who are like embedded in those situations.

208
00:19:34,480 --> 00:19:40,630
So, like for example, if the Steam Deck has a regression, it will come from a Valve engineer through AMD, and it will be reported by that method.

209
00:19:40,630 --> 00:19:49,470
It won't necessarily come to us, but often you will just get an email on the list from someone randomly testing an RC on their laptop and saying, "Hey, this RC doesn't work."

210
00:19:49,470 --> 00:19:55,830
Another layer is distributions will often take the RC kernels and package them, and people will be running the bleeding edge distribution, and then they will see it.

211
00:19:55,830 --> 00:20:01,350
And the distribution will say, "Oh, this kernel seems to have broken a bunch of workloads or a bunch of laptops."

212
00:20:01,350 --> 00:20:04,670
And sometimes it'll just be me sitting here booting it on my laptop going, "Hey, my laptop's not working."

213
00:20:04,680 --> 00:20:06,630
Those are rare.

214
00:20:06,630 --> 00:20:18,750
And And Linus is often uh Linus will have a specific graphics card from 10 years ago, and that graphics card broke, and Linus is the first person to find it because he just happens to be the one person that's in that line that has that card.

215
00:20:18,750 --> 00:20:22,550
So, it's yeah, they come from a lot of places.

216
00:20:22,560 --> 00:20:25,990
Looking for a way to stand out in a competitive job market?

217
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:36,590
Whether you're trying to land your first contributor role or prove you can deliver, take the next step in your career with the new software professional certification from the IEEE Computer Society.

218
00:20:36,600 --> 00:20:43,950
Sign up today at computer.org/certification.

219
00:20:45,390 --> 00:20:50,990
So, in a minute we're going to talk about many of the tools you use and a few more of the development and maintenance processes that you follow.

220
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:59,350
But before we go into that phase of our conversation, I wanted to read a quote from your blog that I thought was really thought-provoking.

221
00:20:59,350 --> 00:21:06,870
So, you wrote, "A warning then to anyone wishing for more vendor code sharing between operating systems.

222
00:21:06,870 --> 00:21:10,750
It generally doesn't end with Linux being better off. That's really thought-provoking.

223
00:21:10,750 --> 00:21:12,990
Can you share a little bit more about what you meant by that, Dave?

224
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:21,910
The thing about what people often don't understand is the incentives for companies to do things cause results that are probably not what you expected.

225
00:21:21,920 --> 00:21:35,710
So, you pushing a company to say we want Linux support for this device, in your mind, produces a Linux driver for this device that is well-written, upstream, maintained piece of code that merges well with the kernel.

226
00:21:35,710 --> 00:21:45,950
But, when you pass that into a company that is writing Windows drivers, their first instinct is how can we share the code between our Windows driver and our Linux driver to cut the cost of doing this. In their mind, that's cutting the cost.

227
00:21:45,950 --> 00:21:49,310
In the real world, it probably doesn't, but companies work like companies do.

228
00:21:49,310 --> 00:21:53,390
So, often what will happen is they will try to put a hardware abstraction layer in.

229
00:21:53,400 --> 00:21:57,310
They will try and port their Windows driver to Linux.

230
00:21:57,310 --> 00:21:59,670
They won't start a rewrite.

231
00:21:59,670 --> 00:22:09,670
So, the results you will get will either be a very second-class driver or a driver that can't go upstream or a driver that needs an impedance layer between the actual vendor and the upstream.

232
00:22:09,670 --> 00:22:16,110
So, you don't always get the results you envision when you say I want more Linux code or I want to use more Windows alignment.

233
00:22:16,110 --> 00:22:24,110
So, it's often good to make sure you don't just let the company run free with that idea because the results you'll get 6 months or a year later won't be what you wanted.

234
00:22:24,120 --> 00:22:36,270
So, it sounds like what you're saying is that in some way the Linux kernel itself might become more fragmented or even harder to support when you start bringing in this vendored code. Is that the right way to think about it?

235
00:22:36,280 --> 00:22:41,430
Yeah, and it just becomes harder for us to extract commonality from the code.

236
00:22:41,430 --> 00:22:50,430
In the kernel, one of the big advantages of the Linux kernel is always is that because everything is in the same tree, we do extract commonality quite often.

237
00:22:50,440 --> 00:23:00,510
The commonality we extract is from our group of drivers, but when you're working in a world where you've got your commonalities between your Windows and your Linux drivers, you have a different vector for extracting that commonality.

238
00:23:00,520 --> 00:23:06,070
You want to abstract the Linux stuff away and keep the common stuff between your Linux and Windows code.

239
00:23:06,070 --> 00:23:11,910
But when the kernel, we want to extract the common stuff out of all the drivers to be in Linux and not in the drivers.

240
00:23:11,910 --> 00:23:17,630
And there's a mismatch between your Linux and Windows, that often gets very difficult for companies to resolve.

241
00:23:17,630 --> 00:23:22,710
So, yeah, we tend to try to say we want a Linux driver because we want to be able to have it optimal in Linux.

242
00:23:22,710 --> 00:23:24,750
And if we see commonalities, we can extract them.

243
00:23:24,760 --> 00:23:28,190
I think the best example I could give you was wireless.

244
00:23:28,190 --> 00:23:34,190
Years ago, there was a big thing with 802.11 layer, and every driver was bringing their own 802.11 layers to the party.

245
00:23:34,190 --> 00:23:37,030
And someone said, "Well, why isn't the operating system providing an 802.11 layer?"

246
00:23:37,030 --> 00:23:43,670
And they did, but still for years we got wireless drivers which were written with the old 802.11 in mind.

247
00:23:43,670 --> 00:23:47,630
So, those things we see different vectors for commonality than Windows Linux vendors do.

248
00:23:47,640 --> 00:23:49,270
Okay, thanks.

249
00:23:49,270 --> 00:23:51,190
That was a really good example.

250
00:23:51,190 --> 00:23:54,590
Now, again, in a moment we're going to talk about patching and backporting and things of that nature.

251
00:23:54,600 --> 00:24:00,950
But you also have just hinted at some of the counterintuitive aspects of maintaining the Linux kernel.

252
00:24:00,950 --> 00:24:09,750
And I know that many of our listeners may know mostly about open source software through GitHub or GitLab or other systems of that nature.

253
00:24:09,750 --> 00:24:19,110
Before we go into the next phase of our show, can you say anything about how Linux kernel maintenance might not work well with the existing model that you would have on GitHub?

254
00:24:19,120 --> 00:24:22,390
I think it just comes down to what I said earlier, scale.

255
00:24:22,390 --> 00:24:30,670
I think the kernel process is just so vast and has so many people that scaling it up through a single tree would be close to impossible.

256
00:24:30,670 --> 00:24:38,910
So, uh the Git Forge model of like there's a single central tree that you send pull requests to just doesn't scale well for us. And that's I think what it comes down to. There is also another side to it.

257
00:24:38,910 --> 00:24:45,390
We have a strong resistance against using proprietary tooling at all in the in the Linux development process.

258
00:24:45,390 --> 00:24:47,230
That isn't a top-down Linus-driven thing.

259
00:24:47,230 --> 00:24:52,630
Linus is very flexible on this, but a lot of the maintainers have personal strong feelings on these things.

260
00:24:52,630 --> 00:24:56,310
So, we tend to shy away from using anything proprietary in the development process.

261
00:24:56,320 --> 00:25:02,950
So, GitHub for example, although it uses Git, is still quite a proprietary system.

262
00:25:02,950 --> 00:25:04,470
In the DRM subsystem, we started use GitLab more extensively.

263
00:25:04,470 --> 00:25:08,830
We use GitLab for hosting a lot of our trees and stuff like that.

264
00:25:08,830 --> 00:25:16,110
So, yeah, I think the scale is generally the biggest problem and the tooling like we're seeing problems with our tooling as well.

265
00:25:16,110 --> 00:25:18,710
Email and stuff doesn't work like it did 20 years ago.

266
00:25:18,710 --> 00:25:21,150
Gmail holds all of Gmail doesn't want to talk to you, your email server is no use.

267
00:25:21,150 --> 00:25:26,430
So, there's a lot of problems we have to solve ourselves that just because the Git forges don't solve them, doesn't mean we don't have to solve them.

268
00:25:26,430 --> 00:25:30,630
And we're working on that with a bunch of tooling at the moment. So, it's a different aspect to it.

269
00:25:30,640 --> 00:25:32,350
Okay, that makes sense.

270
00:25:32,350 --> 00:25:38,710
Now, before you mentioned the idea of a release candidate and we talked about RC1 through RC6.

271
00:25:38,710 --> 00:25:42,710
I have the understanding that there's also something called a stable kernel.

272
00:25:42,710 --> 00:25:45,070
What's a stable kernel and how does that connect to an RC?

273
00:25:45,080 --> 00:25:50,630
So, the stable kernel was something Greg Kroah-Hartman decided to work on. I can't remember, maybe it's 10 years ago.

274
00:25:50,640 --> 00:25:55,030
He'll he'll correct me if I'm wrong someday.

275
00:25:55,030 --> 00:25:57,550
But, the idea was that Linux's releases are all very well and good,

276
00:25:57,550 --> 00:26:07,750
but people want to stay on an older release for longer and still get security fixes and regression fixes and possibly hardware fixes and improvements.

277
00:26:07,750 --> 00:26:13,390
Maybe not major functionality improvements, but quite often they want like a good baseline so they can build things on.

278
00:26:13,390 --> 00:26:21,310
It all this comes from Android world and devices where you don't want to be upgrading the kernel for fear of being sideswiped by a regression in some other area.

279
00:26:21,310 --> 00:26:23,030
So, you just want to keep going on the same kernel, but build out on it.

280
00:26:23,040 --> 00:26:26,110
So, the idea of the stable kernel was shipped.

281
00:26:26,110 --> 00:26:30,750
No one said it would be a great idea, but Greg pushed through and shipped it. It has it comes out I don't know what Greg's release schedules are.

282
00:26:30,760 --> 00:26:40,870
I think he does one every week or two as well and there's a maintenance of about maybe four or five different stable kernels over the range of the last maybe 10 or 20.

283
00:26:40,870 --> 00:26:48,230
Every so often Greg picks a kernel as the LTS, which is the long-term stable release and that kernel will get maintained by Greg or someone else.

284
00:26:48,230 --> 00:26:50,670
Often some someone else gets handed that job.

285
00:26:50,670 --> 00:26:58,750
One of the The things of the stable kernel that was kind of announced was that maintainers should not be required to add to their process to enable the stable kernel,

286
00:26:58,760 --> 00:27:05,270
that the stable kernel should try to not force maintainers to support it, so that we have an option.

287
00:27:05,270 --> 00:27:06,790
I can't say that was good or bad in the graphics subsystem.

288
00:27:06,800 --> 00:27:10,350
We are both good and bad at helping the stable kernel.

289
00:27:10,350 --> 00:27:11,950
Some parts of us are really good and some parts are not.

290
00:27:11,950 --> 00:27:14,470
We don't have a great system.

291
00:27:14,470 --> 00:27:19,070
We don't do bespoke work for stable in our group generally.

292
00:27:19,070 --> 00:27:29,390
We let the stable maintainers take care of it as much as possible, and occasionally if we see a patch that we know should go into stable, we will tag it for that, and people in the hierarchy know to tag certain things for stable if it's a regression from two kernels ago.

293
00:27:29,400 --> 00:27:37,070
And often we will automatically add fixes lines and tag stable on things we think, and then let them decide whether there's something that should go into it or not.

294
00:27:37,070 --> 00:27:40,310
But the rules around stable patches are pretty strict, but they're not that flexible.

295
00:27:40,310 --> 00:27:46,990
They usually should be less than 100 lines, and it should be quite self-contained, so we don't like to get series into the stable kernel.

296
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:50,830
So you just mentioned the next word that I wanted to talk a little bit more.

297
00:27:50,830 --> 00:27:52,870
You talked about the word patches.

298
00:27:52,870 --> 00:27:58,870
Can you say precisely what is a patch and how do you manage a patch when you're doing Linux kernel maintenance?

299
00:27:58,880 --> 00:28:03,030
Yeah, that's a good question. Years ago I don't think I told what I had to answer,

300
00:28:03,030 --> 00:28:14,350
but in the merge request world, it's a different way of working as in the kernel we treat a patch as the smallest unit of work to make a change, but be very self-contained.

301
00:28:14,350 --> 00:28:18,430
We put a lot of effort into our patch summary Yeah, got it.

302
00:28:18,430 --> 00:28:21,070
Text in the patch because the commit message is actually very important from the kernel.

303
00:28:21,070 --> 00:28:24,470
A lot of other merge request flow people put a lot of work into their merge requests.

304
00:28:24,480 --> 00:28:28,910
We put that work as much as possible into the commit messages for each patch.

305
00:28:28,920 --> 00:28:31,950
Each patch should be very self-contained.

306
00:28:31,950 --> 00:28:33,710
It should do a single thing.

307
00:28:33,710 --> 00:28:39,630
We It should not be If your commit message starts having and and but, then you're like, "Okay, maybe this should be two patches."

308
00:28:39,630 --> 00:28:42,990
So you get the instincts for what a patch should be over years of writing patches.

309
00:28:42,990 --> 00:28:46,510
You should not drop a whole new driver in one patch.

310
00:28:46,510 --> 00:28:52,310
You should try and drop infrastructure changes needed for your driver, then the core of the driver, then add features to the driver, and they should all be well explained.

311
00:28:52,310 --> 00:28:55,510
I suppose the other way to think of a patch is it's the unit for review.

312
00:28:55,510 --> 00:28:59,470
This is something that should be digestible by a reviewer,

313
00:28:59,470 --> 00:29:08,390
and they should be able to make a decision on a patch-by-patch basis whether there's something that we should accept into the kernel or something we shouldn't accept in the kernel or are there some problems when we need to iterate on again.

314
00:29:08,390 --> 00:29:20,070
And then a patch series is a gathering of those patches with a cover letter that describes the overall idea of where you're going to go with the series, and then each patch then will take you on the journey to the result that you want.

315
00:29:20,080 --> 00:29:22,030
Okay, that makes sense.

316
00:29:22,030 --> 00:29:27,870
Can you tell us are there some tools that you specifically use when it comes to managing a patch or a series of patches?

317
00:29:27,880 --> 00:29:30,030
And then how do you use those tools?

318
00:29:30,040 --> 00:29:37,430
Like everything, there's a lot of tools, and they're all different ones, and every kernel maintainer probably has their own set.

319
00:29:37,430 --> 00:29:39,750
I think the current core is Git.

320
00:29:39,750 --> 00:29:42,790
Everyone nearly has agreed, like years ago we had other systems, but Git is the current core.

321
00:29:42,790 --> 00:29:47,550
You generally will develop all of your changes in a Git tree on your local machine.

322
00:29:47,550 --> 00:29:56,390
You will When you're finished out with the development, you'll have to go back and probably make a patch series out of that development that is linear and sane, because often development is not linear and sane,

323
00:29:56,390 --> 00:30:06,150
so you have to go back and distill your patch series into something linear and sane, then go through the process of making sure it's submittable, making sure you're following the rules, that it actually is patch series you want to send out.

324
00:30:06,150 --> 00:30:08,870
And then you go through the process of sending it to the mailing list.

325
00:30:08,870 --> 00:30:10,510
That's where we use that through email.

326
00:30:10,510 --> 00:30:13,470
We have a new tool called B4.

327
00:30:13,470 --> 00:30:15,910
We have a set tool for sending patch series out.

328
00:30:15,910 --> 00:30:26,630
There are also other patch management systems, so Git isn't always the best thing to manage patches with, so there's a number of things series of tools called Quilt, there's a thing called StGit.

329
00:30:26,630 --> 00:30:30,790
So, depending on how you want to manage your patches, there are other alternate tools.

330
00:30:30,790 --> 00:30:43,310
I personally, when I'm writing patch series, and I don't write a lot of patch series for the kernel these days, will use just Git trees, and I will keep the cover letter just to a side, but that's only because I'm probably only ever working on one major patch series at a time anymore.

331
00:30:43,320 --> 00:30:46,190
I'm not doing a lot of streams of work at that level.

332
00:30:46,200 --> 00:30:51,950
Okay, so you have patches and you have patch series and I'm glad you defined each of those terms.

333
00:30:51,950 --> 00:30:56,190
I also quickly wanted to talk about the concept of a backport.

334
00:30:56,190 --> 00:30:59,510
What is a backport and how does it connect to the topics we've discussed so far?

335
00:30:59,520 --> 00:31:04,270
So a backport is probably a many aspects of what constitutes a backport.

336
00:31:04,270 --> 00:31:11,110
So you can consider a patch going into stable being a backport as in at the simplest term a backport is something that

337
00:31:11,110 --> 00:31:17,630
you have to maybe adapt to an older kernel as opposed to just apply to an older kernel.

338
00:31:17,630 --> 00:31:27,310
So if you have a patch that goes into like the latest kernel and you want to apply it to a kernel that's five kernels old, sometimes that patch will just apply and it's fine and that's a technically a backport but it's not really I've not done anything.

339
00:31:27,310 --> 00:31:34,390
But if you actually have to modify that patch to make it adapted to the older kernel API or just the older state of the driver back then,

340
00:31:34,390 --> 00:31:45,270
that's more getting to what an actual backport is because say you have a security fix or something in the new kernel and it's the same security problem exists in the older kernel but it's in a very different state.

341
00:31:45,270 --> 00:31:49,830
Like the code that causes has been changed substantially but there's still underlying security problems there.

342
00:31:49,830 --> 00:31:53,430
Well then you need to adapt that fix for that kernel and sometimes it's a full rewrite.

343
00:31:53,430 --> 00:31:56,270
Sometimes it's just a concept you're applying back but you still need to do it.

344
00:31:56,270 --> 00:32:02,791
You then have to scale that up again if you're looking into and I'll talk about about Red Hat works. So Red Hat has a rail kernel.

345
00:32:02,791 --> 00:32:05,430
We backport subsystems.

346
00:32:05,430 --> 00:32:17,510
So like we will take the full DRM subsystem which is like thousands of lines of code and patches and backport that whole on mass to a kernel that was maybe eight kernels old.

347
00:32:17,510 --> 00:32:21,110
That's like that's extreme backporting and you see Ubuntu do the same.

348
00:32:21,110 --> 00:32:23,030
You'll see a lot of stable kernels for devices, Android do a bunch of it.

349
00:32:23,040 --> 00:32:36,230
So backporting is kind of like I won't say the dirty secret but if you're actually releasing a product of something using the Linux kernel, you would generally be in the market for having to backport things to that kernel and need your engineers to do that.

350
00:32:36,240 --> 00:32:41,431
How does backporting connect to the idea of doing a revert? What's a revert then?

351
00:32:41,440 --> 00:32:45,470
So, a revert is simply just I found a regression.

352
00:32:45,470 --> 00:32:51,590
We need to get rid of that patch out of the tree, but Git doesn't allow you to change history, so you just revert the patch.

353
00:32:51,590 --> 00:32:56,710
You take the patch that's in the tree and you apply the reverse patch, and that's it gone from that tree.

354
00:32:56,710 --> 00:32:59,630
Then you have to make sure that the revert is also treated just like any other patch.

355
00:32:59,630 --> 00:33:02,910
It goes into the stable cycle that makes sure it gets removed from the older stable trees.

356
00:33:02,920 --> 00:33:04,790
Backports should pick up the revert.

357
00:33:04,790 --> 00:33:11,590
So, generally reverts have to have like will have a tagline for what they're reverting, so you know exactly this patch reverses this patch.

358
00:33:11,600 --> 00:33:13,230
Okay, that was awesome.

359
00:33:13,230 --> 00:33:17,070
So, now we've covered a lot of the key details and terms.

360
00:33:17,070 --> 00:33:24,950
And if listeners want to know more, they're welcome to check in the show notes and we'll provide links to kernel.org articles and handbooks and things of that nature.

361
00:33:24,950 --> 00:33:31,270
What I wanted to pick up on next was something that you mentioned just a moment ago, which were issues about security concerns.

362
00:33:31,270 --> 00:33:40,510
Can you tell us a little bit about how you do security reviews or maybe talk a little bit about how you do performance or stability reviews in the Linux kernel maintenance process?

363
00:33:40,520 --> 00:33:44,630
I'll deal with security first cuz it's kind of a special case in some ways.

364
00:33:44,630 --> 00:33:49,950
So, we have a dedicated kernel security team. I'm not on that team. It's Greg is probably one of the leaders on that.

365
00:33:49,950 --> 00:33:51,630
I don't know who else is involved.

366
00:33:51,630 --> 00:33:53,910
There's an embargo process, but it's very quick.

367
00:33:53,920 --> 00:33:58,870
I think 7 days is what Linus likes to give people.

368
00:33:58,870 --> 00:34:00,870
Linus is very vocal about security fixes just being fixes.

369
00:34:00,870 --> 00:34:10,470
He believes there's a lot of theater in security, so he's very pushy on just getting fixes into history as quickly as possible, and that's it's a fix for a bug.

370
00:34:10,470 --> 00:34:12,190
We need to get it upstream and send it out.

371
00:34:12,190 --> 00:34:23,909
But we've built up more like Greg is building up like a CVE sort of handling system for the kernel, where our own CVE handler, but we have a lot of potential security fixes in the kernel.

372
00:34:23,909 --> 00:34:32,070
And over the last year you may have seen some news articles and discussion about that, and like you know, we're we can never really say a lot of things for sure if they're a security problem.

373
00:34:32,070 --> 00:34:38,710
Because it's a security problem in the kernel, it may not be your security problem in your kernel, and you need to be in charge of that.

374
00:34:38,710 --> 00:34:43,909
So, if you're a downstream of the kernel packaging it up in in your operating system, you probably need to be more on top of this.

375
00:34:43,909 --> 00:34:52,830
That the The upstream kernel is used in so many different ways that every specific CVE that we issue for the kernel may not apply to your use case.

376
00:34:52,830 --> 00:34:59,510
But, the subtlety in the security industry isn't quite there yet in that there's a lot of CV scanners that just take blanket approaches.

377
00:34:59,510 --> 00:35:03,830
There's a lot of, you know, so security area is definitely a tricky one.

378
00:35:03,830 --> 00:35:05,510
In terms of process, generally there's a security mailing list.

379
00:35:05,510 --> 00:35:10,510
If it's in your area, you will get CC'd on the report.

380
00:35:10,510 --> 00:35:13,270
You're expected to bring in the other people in your area who are responsible.

381
00:35:13,270 --> 00:35:14,910
So, you know, I can get the top-level CC.

382
00:35:14,910 --> 00:35:19,510
If it's not something I directly could fix or know about, I'll have to bring in people from the people involved.

383
00:35:19,510 --> 00:35:21,190
And, you know, and then we'll keep it secret for 7 days.

384
00:35:21,190 --> 00:35:24,430
If we can't If we can get a patch out, we'll get a patch out.

385
00:35:24,430 --> 00:35:29,831
But, yeah, generally it Lately, I think AI-generated reports have sort of saturating the bandwidth as well. Some of them are good.

386
00:35:29,831 --> 00:35:32,710
Some of them are bad, but the problem is it's bandwidth saturation problem.

387
00:35:32,710 --> 00:35:36,950
You know, the bad ones will still cause you to waste time.

388
00:35:36,950 --> 00:35:46,750
In terms of dealing with other types of regression like performance issues or things like that, quite often they just sort of come up over time.

389
00:35:46,750 --> 00:35:56,070
Like, we do try to do In the last few years, we've moved into CI a lot more, but one of the biggest challenges of the Linux development process is it doesn't really integrate well with how CI works.

390
00:35:56,070 --> 00:36:08,791
And, it's where I quite like having I also work on some other projects where we have full GitLab CI processes where every merge request goes through a full CI run before being, you know, merged. I like that.

391
00:36:08,791 --> 00:36:11,430
It makes life so much easier.

392
00:36:11,430 --> 00:36:16,510
But, the Linux kernel is not designed for that because of our workflow.

393
00:36:16,510 --> 00:36:21,150
There's no like central point to pick these patches up and push them through a centralized CI system.

394
00:36:21,160 --> 00:36:24,310
And, everyone has built bespoke CI.

395
00:36:24,310 --> 00:36:26,350
And, again, a central CI wouldn't scale.

396
00:36:26,360 --> 00:36:32,430
Like, one of the problems with CI is it like it needs resourcing and if we had a central the scaling is going to kill it.

397
00:36:32,440 --> 00:36:39,071
So, having them per subsystem and having them more bespoke is probably a good thing. We have a graphics sort of CI pipeline. It's not quite there yet.

398
00:36:39,080 --> 00:36:42,070
We're working It's been worked on over the last year.

399
00:36:42,070 --> 00:36:43,790
Some of the Qualcomm being quite pushing on it.

400
00:36:43,790 --> 00:36:54,030
The idea is that I would love to see a pre-merge CI system that I would know that when a patch set gets to me it's gone through some sort of CI, but quite often that's not true today.

401
00:36:54,030 --> 00:37:05,470
A lot of Intel have done a sort of a CI integration with the mailing list, which is janky, but it works as in they get patches on the mailing list, they apply them to a tree and they push them through to CI and they report out through a tool called patchwork.

402
00:37:05,470 --> 00:37:13,030
I know AMD have some internal CI that they will push their next kernels through as well and it's kind of very patchy the coverage in that area, but often that's how we,

403
00:37:13,030 --> 00:37:21,190
you know, initially we'll find regressions and things like that are through that, but then Valve will tell us if this they updated the new Steam Deck and it's suddenly slower.

404
00:37:21,190 --> 00:37:23,510
You know, things like that will come through.

405
00:37:23,510 --> 00:37:30,510
Also, a lot of regressions in performance and stuff can be it can look like it's a graphics problem, but it's actually a power management problem because,

406
00:37:30,510 --> 00:37:37,950
you know, they're so tightly integrated now all the pieces of your your SOCs and your GPUs is that there's so many pieces to them.

407
00:37:37,950 --> 00:37:41,710
Yeah, you could suddenly have a clock change and yeah, that can cause some problems.

408
00:37:41,710 --> 00:37:46,070
So, it's CI it's it's also you have to know the architecture of the whole system to track those down.

409
00:37:46,080 --> 00:37:49,750
Thanks, that's helpful. I wanted to pick up on two phrases that you used.

410
00:37:49,750 --> 00:37:55,830
You mentioned CI and I know there's something called kernelci.org and before that you mentioned B4.

411
00:37:55,830 --> 00:38:02,870
Can you tell our listeners very specifically like what are the things you run in CI and then how do tools like B4 help you?

412
00:38:02,880 --> 00:38:06,870
Do you run them in CI or do you run them on your developer workstations?

413
00:38:06,870 --> 00:38:08,390
What's the process there, Dave?

414
00:38:08,400 --> 00:38:13,910
Yeah, there's no good central standard here at all.

415
00:38:13,910 --> 00:38:18,630
So, I'll go B4 is a tool that is replaces email for getting patches from you to the mailing lists.

416
00:38:18,640 --> 00:38:23,750
We've the two kernel tools group, Constantine is the main guy there.

417
00:38:23,750 --> 00:38:34,350
I have developed a thing called public inbox, which is a mailing list archiver holster that we use as kind of a repository of a database of all of the patches ever kind of sent to any mailing list.

418
00:38:34,350 --> 00:38:38,950
Everything is in this public inbox sort of hierarchy.

419
00:38:38,950 --> 00:38:43,550
And B4 is a tool then that lets you submit things to those inboxes.

420
00:38:43,550 --> 00:38:48,990
In reverse, there's a tool called LEI, which is for pulling things out and lets you query those inboxes.

421
00:38:48,990 --> 00:38:55,270
So, I use an example to query for all the pull requests in the last week, get them into a local mailbox, and then I process them locally.

422
00:38:55,270 --> 00:39:01,670
So, it's just tooling that avoids using SMTP as an email server and lets you go beyond that.

423
00:39:01,670 --> 00:39:06,630
But, in terms of projects like Kernel CI and we have a GitLab CI project on freedesktop.

424
00:39:06,640 --> 00:39:10,310
There's not much sort of coherence there, I suppose.

425
00:39:10,310 --> 00:39:14,790
That's why different subsystems kind of use them in vague ways.

426
00:39:14,790 --> 00:39:18,070
There's a little bit more like it's generally after-the-fact CI as well.

427
00:39:18,070 --> 00:39:22,230
It's generally stuff goes through CI after it's been merged to Linux tree, after it's been put in next.

428
00:39:22,230 --> 00:39:28,790
There's very rarely we have CI in advance of the patches going into someone's tree. Yeah, and that's something I'd like to change.

429
00:39:28,800 --> 00:39:34,630
Like I encourage people in my area to try and develop things that to help me change that.

430
00:39:34,630 --> 00:39:42,030
I probably ain't I'm not the person to do that, but I definitely encouraged and open to workflows that allow that to happen because I see the value in it.

431
00:39:42,030 --> 00:39:49,990
But, yeah, in terms of overall, like Kernel CI has has the name, but in terms of whether all of the kernel goes through it is not really that doesn't really actually happen.

432
00:39:49,990 --> 00:39:54,350
And again, you know, we also had a a number of robots over the year.

433
00:39:54,350 --> 00:40:01,550
Intel had a a tool called Zero-Day, which was something that would apply patches to it, but sometimes Zero-Day would be You never knew when Zero-Day was going to respond to you.

434
00:40:01,550 --> 00:40:07,750
Zero-Day could tell you about your patch a week later, it could tell you an hour later, it could tell you a month later. A lot of these again are after-the-fact things.

435
00:40:07,750 --> 00:40:11,430
You send something to the mailing list and maybe you get a response.

436
00:40:11,430 --> 00:40:18,190
Trying to bring the window of timing on those and trying to, you know, constrain those again is hard because you know, there's so many kernel patches and scaling is hard.

437
00:40:18,190 --> 00:40:22,870
So, the Intel CI is probably one of the has lasted quite a while in that they get patches on the mailing list.

438
00:40:22,870 --> 00:40:25,591
I see them apply. I see them runs.

439
00:40:25,591 --> 00:40:27,350
And it just it works.

440
00:40:27,350 --> 00:40:29,230
But, it's very strong together with your baling twine and stuff.

441
00:40:29,230 --> 00:40:30,950
It's not a very coherent system.

442
00:40:30,960 --> 00:40:36,230
Thanks for those clarifying comments about how you're using tools and how you're using CI.

443
00:40:36,230 --> 00:40:38,350
I did want to follow up with one final question in this area.

444
00:40:38,350 --> 00:40:48,190
Do you use various types of fuzzing techniques when it comes to the DRM subsystem, or is fuzzing used in any other parts of reviewing patches in the Linux kernel?

445
00:40:48,200 --> 00:40:52,510
Fuzzing seems to go through like like popularity phases, I suppose.

446
00:40:52,510 --> 00:40:54,270
It becomes popular for a while.

447
00:40:54,270 --> 00:40:56,830
They find all the fuzzing bugs, and then it stops.

448
00:40:56,830 --> 00:41:03,510
We don't have anything specific in our subsystem for it, but there's a upstream project called syzkaller.

449
00:41:03,510 --> 00:41:10,910
Syzkaller is notorious for finding the weirdest race conditions in the weirdest places in that and then providing you with very detailed information on how it did that.

450
00:41:10,920 --> 00:41:17,830
It literally intelligently spams all of the kernel APIs in various orders with various random things.

451
00:41:17,830 --> 00:41:21,030
Like, so it's not fuzzing in the sense of it's sending complete garbage to things.

452
00:41:21,030 --> 00:41:23,270
It knows the structure of the ioctl interfaces.

453
00:41:23,270 --> 00:41:26,070
It knows the structure of the system call interfaces.

454
00:41:26,070 --> 00:41:29,870
So, it knows it needs to pass in valid file descriptors to get past.

455
00:41:29,870 --> 00:41:31,550
Cuz otherwise, 99% of your fuzzing stops at the first block.

456
00:41:31,550 --> 00:41:35,190
So, it's intelligent enough to try and get down into the kernel depth.

457
00:41:35,190 --> 00:41:39,790
It's a quite a cool project, but yeah, we don't specifically use that, but we respond to reports of that.

458
00:41:39,790 --> 00:41:49,710
But again, it comes down to you can get overwhelmed with auto-generated reports of syzkaller problems because some of them are so deep and so niche that like no user is ever going to hit these. They can often get pushed to the background.

459
00:41:49,710 --> 00:41:51,590
They can be that one-in-a-million problem.

460
00:41:51,590 --> 00:41:55,830
And that's good to know, but sometimes we have to over-prioritize them, and that's often a bad thing.

461
00:41:55,840 --> 00:41:58,990
Okay, that was really helpful.

