468
00:42:28,350 --> 00:42:32,270
So, I wanted to talk briefly about how people get involved with the Linux kernel maintenance process.

469
00:42:32,270 --> 00:42:39,590
I know you have this idea of new people submitting a patch or making a suggestion for a change.

470
00:42:39,590 --> 00:42:45,590
How does it work if one of our listeners finds what they think is a bug or has an idea for a performance improvement?

471
00:42:45,590 --> 00:42:48,070
Can you give us a few insights on what that process might look like?

472
00:42:48,080 --> 00:42:49,870
The world has changed over the years.

473
00:42:49,870 --> 00:42:59,910
I think the best way to get involved now is to get a job where they tell you to get involved, but secondary to that, if you have an your personal issues are generally the best way to start on something.

474
00:42:59,910 --> 00:43:06,670
If it's a problem you have on your laptop or in your system that you're seeing, then yeah, you have a sort of an incentive to want to go down that road and dig into it.

475
00:43:06,670 --> 00:43:08,270
And it's the code's all there.

476
00:43:08,270 --> 00:43:10,590
I encourage people to go out look into it.

477
00:43:10,590 --> 00:43:12,431
Like, use your ChatGPT or whatever. Use Gemini.

478
00:43:12,431 --> 00:43:17,190
Use those to even interrogate what you think about the code or trying to figure it out.

479
00:43:17,190 --> 00:43:22,470
Work through If you find the area of the kernel the problem is in, well, then find the community attached to that area.

480
00:43:22,470 --> 00:43:25,710
There is no single Linux kernel community.

481
00:43:25,720 --> 00:43:27,350
There's the Linux kernel mailing list.

482
00:43:27,360 --> 00:43:29,270
That is not the place you start.

483
00:43:29,270 --> 00:43:34,110
The Linux kernel mailing list is where you drop patches that kind of you aren't sure what to do with.

484
00:43:34,110 --> 00:43:35,750
And very often it will get ignored.

485
00:43:35,750 --> 00:43:37,550
It's more of a It's not a central place for development.

486
00:43:37,550 --> 00:43:39,190
You have to find your community.

487
00:43:39,190 --> 00:43:41,950
So, if you want to work in the graphics, you go, "Okay, well, this is a DRM subsystem.

488
00:43:41,960 --> 00:43:44,350
Where are those developers?

489
00:43:44,350 --> 00:43:49,511
Are they on They're on their DRM GitLab or they're on You know, can I find them on IRC maybe? Can I find them on Discord?

490
00:43:49,511 --> 00:43:55,030
Are they Is there a community of people I can actually talk to and say, 'Is this a good idea?'"

491
00:43:55,030 --> 00:43:57,590
You could send a mail mail to the maintainer.

492
00:43:57,590 --> 00:43:59,270
Quite often, you will likely get a reply.

493
00:43:59,270 --> 00:44:03,910
Maintainer dependent because some of us just get so many emails that they don't all We don't get to them all.

494
00:44:03,910 --> 00:44:12,870
I try to get to a lot of the new one new people asking questions by diverting them to the person who could better answer that question in in the area, but not all of it is a steady thing.

495
00:44:12,870 --> 00:44:22,670
But yeah, find a community, find out how they work, lurk for a while, like maybe watch their workflows a bit, maybe read a few patches, maybe get an like get an idea of how the patches in that subsystem are reviewed,

496
00:44:22,670 --> 00:44:27,270
maybe review a few patches if you're feeling that you know enough C and you've learned enough about the area, you know.

497
00:44:27,280 --> 00:44:30,590
So like you can start by You can always do a drive-by patch, right?

498
00:44:30,590 --> 00:44:33,630
That's where you just drop the patch and hope someone applies it and never look at it again.

499
00:44:33,640 --> 00:44:36,270
And that's often for small things, that's probably fine.

500
00:44:36,270 --> 00:44:46,270
But if something you're actually interested in getting into kernel development, that's often the best way to start is find your personal itch that you want to scratch, and then work with the community to figure out how to do that.

501
00:44:46,270 --> 00:44:51,230
I do get off asked a lot "Can you give me a project to work on or something to start me off?"

502
00:44:51,230 --> 00:44:58,390
And that's a tough question because without knowing your level of ability and skill, without having mentored you on something, I can't really give you that.

503
00:44:58,390 --> 00:45:01,430
We've tried to construct to-do lists, and they they work in some ways.

504
00:45:01,430 --> 00:45:09,470
We've had some success with keeping to-do lists upstream as long as we maintain those and I give sort of levels of easy versus hard.

505
00:45:09,470 --> 00:45:15,790
But quite often you find like if an experienced developer hits a roadblock that is just a simple to-do list item, they're going to go straight through it.

506
00:45:15,790 --> 00:45:17,350
It'll be a patch 10 minutes later.

507
00:45:17,350 --> 00:45:23,830
So you have to have things that core developers don't want to fix right now because otherwise they would just fix them right now cuz they're important.

508
00:45:23,830 --> 00:45:33,750
So it's like it's hard to find that balance of tasks and you know, and we often have new projects that are up and coming where there's like a the project has started, like our Nova project, which you know, is a GPU driver that's started.

509
00:45:33,750 --> 00:45:40,710
It's like people want to get involved, but it's like it's kind of at a problem stage where we can't get people involved because we haven't opened up the window where it's parallelizable.

510
00:45:40,710 --> 00:45:42,670
Right now it's very serial.

511
00:45:42,670 --> 00:45:45,870
We need to get certain things done, and we need to get to a baseline, and we haven't got there yet.

512
00:45:45,870 --> 00:45:52,910
So it's hard to get people to join until that baseline's achieved unless they know some of the the deeper parts of it are are working on it full-time.

513
00:45:52,920 --> 00:46:01,030
Thanks, that's really helpful. Now, a while ago you mentioned the idea of a patch series as having like a cover letter.

514
00:46:01,030 --> 00:46:10,390
So what goes along with a patch series beyond the cover letter, how do you show like what its tags are, how it was tested, how it was verified, what fuzzing it went through?

515
00:46:10,390 --> 00:46:14,870
Can you give us a little bit more of a sense of what a patch series looks like for the Linux kernel?

516
00:46:14,880 --> 00:46:20,590
Honestly, essentially a patch of letter, none of that generally goes into them, unfortunately.

517
00:46:20,590 --> 00:46:27,430
I think we would like that to happen more, but often the cover letter is just often it has like the list of I said version.

518
00:46:27,430 --> 00:46:34,830
So, say you sent a patch series out once, it's been reviewed, it's probably hit a few fuzzers, somebody may have gone through a CI system.

519
00:46:34,830 --> 00:46:38,150
The second time you send it out, you may include some of that feedback into the cover letter.

520
00:46:38,150 --> 00:46:48,150
You may say changes since the previous version are because I got this feedback from a reviewer, I got this feedback from an AI reviewer, I got this feedback from a CI system, the Cisco found a problem.

521
00:46:48,150 --> 00:46:52,070
You know, something like that is often in there, but it's not essential.

522
00:46:52,070 --> 00:46:54,950
It's not we don't have to put in that it went through a CI.

523
00:46:54,950 --> 00:47:08,190
Often I say the CI is after the fact, so we reviewed a patch for more like aesthetic and you know, functionality, and then when it's merged, we figure out whether there's actually a major regression we didn't spot. And to honest, we get a we we don't spot them all.

524
00:47:08,200 --> 00:47:15,510
One of the advantages we're seeing with AI reviewers is that they find some of these things that we humans just aren't we might spot them one day and the next day we might not.

525
00:47:15,510 --> 00:47:17,510
And you know, we're not the consistency isn't always there.

526
00:47:17,520 --> 00:47:26,790
So, yeah, it just goes down to ideally yeah, we'd have all the information that's pertinent to the person applying the series to make it easier for them to apply your series.

527
00:47:26,790 --> 00:47:30,590
But in practice, it varies between two lines and essays.

528
00:47:30,600 --> 00:47:32,550
Okay, that's helpful.

529
00:47:32,550 --> 00:47:43,391
I remember before you mentioned the idea of long-term support or LTS, and we've already talked about things like backporting, but we haven't yet defined the word downstream kernel. What does downstream mean?

530
00:47:43,400 --> 00:47:48,910
So, from the kernel's point of view, downstream is any kernel that isn't Linus's, I suppose.

531
00:47:48,910 --> 00:47:52,591
And maybe I can pull it out to being any kernel that isn't stable. So, Linus and Greg's trees.

532
00:47:52,600 --> 00:47:56,630
Anything that's not theirs and isn't feeding into them.

533
00:47:56,630 --> 00:48:01,950
So, I wouldn't consider like the DRM tree being a downstream because we are going to feed our tree into Linus.

534
00:48:01,950 --> 00:48:14,430
But, if you're a consumer of Linus's tree like Red Hat is through Rail, through Fedora, or Android has like you know, a Chrome OS has, Ubuntu.

535
00:48:14,430 --> 00:48:19,790
So, any of those sort of kernels that are not just taking Linus's kernel and packaging them up, those are what we would consider downstream trees.

536
00:48:19,790 --> 00:48:26,790
Any trees that have diverged from the Linux core and added backports of their own, drivers of their own, things that haven't gone upstream of their own.

537
00:48:26,790 --> 00:48:30,110
So, yeah, a bespoke kernel that is for some use case.

538
00:48:30,120 --> 00:48:31,830
Okay, that's really helpful.

539
00:48:31,830 --> 00:48:38,230
Now, one of the other things that I remember you talking about before is what I might call a cross-subsystem change.

540
00:48:38,230 --> 00:48:44,630
So, like for example, you mentioned how power management could influence things that are related to DRM.

541
00:48:44,630 --> 00:48:48,510
And as a long-time Linux user myself, I have had that happen.

542
00:48:48,510 --> 00:48:53,550
So, can you talk a little bit about cross-subsystem changes in the Linux kernel?

543
00:48:53,550 --> 00:48:55,631
And how do you manage that? It seems incredibly complicated.

544
00:48:55,640 --> 00:49:06,110
It's one of the more difficult aspects, I suppose, in some ways to handle because you often get a bug report in through you know, through your handling system and you're then going, "Okay, well, now is this my problem?"

545
00:49:06,110 --> 00:49:14,950
And you go looking for it and you dig through the kernel and you decide it's not your problem, but how do you then tell the person who submitted that bug report, "You need to go annoy these other people."

546
00:49:14,950 --> 00:49:17,870
Subsystems will go and bring that other person other subsystem in and try and talk to them.

547
00:49:17,870 --> 00:49:24,710
Like, we have good relations between the power management people and the you know, the GPU people because we know we need to have those problems worked out.

548
00:49:24,710 --> 00:49:29,790
But, like you know, we don't If somebody comes in and says, "My network card suddenly broke my graphics card."

549
00:49:29,790 --> 00:49:32,550
then it's started That's another level up of how do we figure that out?

550
00:49:32,550 --> 00:49:34,230
Like you know, those sort of problems.

551
00:49:34,230 --> 00:49:40,390
Quite a lot of that though is often mitigated through the core kernel as in like you try to use standard kernel APIs.

552
00:49:40,390 --> 00:49:45,110
You don't try to make cross dependencies between your areas without having them go through some sort of standard core kernel piece.

553
00:49:45,110 --> 00:49:50,510
And often the core kernel piece is then maintained by people on both sides of that sort of divide.

554
00:49:50,510 --> 00:49:55,110
So, we have things like what the DMA buff subsystem, which is like a way of sharing memory between devices.

555
00:49:55,110 --> 00:50:00,190
We have that, so graphics people will use that, but also our DMA people will use that.

556
00:50:00,190 --> 00:50:10,230
And but we will talk through the DMA buff layer, and when we have issues or bugs, we resolve them around that sort of the community around DMA buff will be a slightly different community than the community around the core graphics or the mode setting team.

557
00:50:10,230 --> 00:50:17,630
So, it's a Yeah, we I say with power management has often got a little community around it of people who are both GPU people and power management people.

558
00:50:17,630 --> 00:50:24,150
So, again, it's mostly like a social aspect of trying to get the people to actually talk to each other and trust each other that when they say they have a problem, they believe each other.

559
00:50:24,160 --> 00:50:37,550
So, can you tell a story or give a concrete example of like a some type of cross subsystem change, and then how you as maintainers actually decided, quote unquote, whose fault it was and who actually had to make the fix?

560
00:50:37,560 --> 00:50:41,470
Quite often, we don't hit that many at that level.

561
00:50:41,470 --> 00:50:45,790
Quite often, like big subsystem cross subsystem changes are well planned out.

562
00:50:45,790 --> 00:50:49,310
We will talk about them for maybe could be a a year, could be 6 months out.

563
00:50:49,310 --> 00:50:51,230
Like, you know, we want to make a big change to a kernel API.

564
00:50:51,240 --> 00:50:54,110
It's either something that's very automatic, and I don't ever see it.

565
00:50:54,110 --> 00:50:57,630
It happens completely in someone else's tree.

566
00:50:57,630 --> 00:51:02,950
It goes to Linus, and I All I get is the fallout when I have to fix the conflicts and stuff, but that's just part of the job.

567
00:51:02,950 --> 00:51:08,710
At a more core level, a lot of the time, producing core functionality in the kernel that is cross subsystem is where it gets tricky.

568
00:51:08,720 --> 00:51:20,710
Getting that, like taking like DMA buff into the kernel and then making it usable for everyone, those discussions are harder because you often have a very opposing views on how these things should work,

569
00:51:20,710 --> 00:51:25,630
and some people will take very strident positions and not want to move.

570
00:51:25,630 --> 00:51:36,710
And how do you Our negotiation on those topics is actually quite a lot of my job is impedance mismatching your engineers who are very focused on how they think something should work and engineers who think other things should work.

571
00:51:36,710 --> 00:51:39,710
And it's like, "Yeah, you're both right, but that doesn't solve the problem."

572
00:51:39,710 --> 00:51:42,910
It's like, "I need you to come together and work out how it's going to work between you."

573
00:51:42,910 --> 00:51:45,350
And you get personality issues there.

574
00:51:45,350 --> 00:51:53,230
And again, what part of my job, and I think part of why I like doing a lot of maintenance work I do is is is dealing with the people and just solving those problems between them.

575
00:51:53,230 --> 00:51:55,310
But, that's quite a lot of it.

576
00:51:55,310 --> 00:51:57,150
It's just that social trust aspect is very important.

577
00:51:57,150 --> 00:52:08,470
And again, you find if you build One of our That's what That's come up and Simona talks about this quite often is that when we have developers build things inside companies and then upstream them, we often don't get that commonality.

578
00:52:08,470 --> 00:52:15,790
But, it's not even often about the commonality of the code as much as it's about having the community of the people who understand the problem talking to each other.

579
00:52:15,790 --> 00:52:26,550
So, we've had this problem with I suppose one of our graphics drivers went a bit rogue a few years ago and did a whole lot of development work internally in the company and then decided they were going to upstream that work.

580
00:52:26,550 --> 00:52:33,390
And when they started upstreaming that work, we started seeing major structural problems with some of the other work they'd been doing leading to that work.

581
00:52:33,390 --> 00:52:37,510
And it was a the driver had diverged maybe by a year or two. You know, we're not closely monitoring.

582
00:52:37,510 --> 00:52:39,230
The trust system is there for a good reason.

583
00:52:39,230 --> 00:52:44,630
But, the problem that came out of that is like they literally that didn't understand the problems that other people were solving.

584
00:52:44,630 --> 00:52:47,390
And again, we often have case where someone will go, "Okay, well, I've got this problem."

585
00:52:47,400 --> 00:52:52,750
"Oh, we tried that 5 years ago and we couldn't do it." It's like, "Well, where did you try it?" "Oh, we did it internally."

586
00:52:52,750 --> 00:52:56,910
And it's like, "Well, if you'd done that in public, we'd have that evidence that you tried it so the other person wouldn't waste their time."

587
00:52:56,920 --> 00:53:04,110
So, it's building up that sort of community infrastructure, especially with new technologies are coming along like memory management, things have changed on GPUs and stuff.

588
00:53:04,110 --> 00:53:12,790
And building up that knowledge base and that core of people that understand the problem with that in the industry is that in the each company there's like one or two of these people.

589
00:53:12,790 --> 00:53:16,270
But, you want all of the industry experts in the same place to solve that for Linux.

590
00:53:16,270 --> 00:53:20,470
And that's getting those five or 10 people to talk and trust each other is the tricky part.

591
00:53:20,480 --> 00:53:29,470
Yeah, thank you for saying that. You've used the word trust several times and you've talked about how this is as much a technical issue as it is a human issue as well.

592
00:53:29,470 --> 00:53:31,550
And that makes me think of perhaps like a bigger picture question.

593
00:53:31,560 --> 00:53:39,150
You know, as you're going through an RC, I'm guessing you must have some rough idea of hey, this RC is really healthy.

594
00:53:39,160 --> 00:53:43,110
It's going well. Or oh, this RC is really struggling.

595
00:53:43,110 --> 00:53:46,190
We're going to have to go to five or six or beyond.

596
00:53:46,190 --> 00:53:52,390
Can you take a big step back and give us a sense of how you're gauging like the overall health of a release cycle?

597
00:53:52,400 --> 00:53:55,830
Linus is really good at this. I'm not as good.

598
00:53:55,830 --> 00:53:58,950
Linus is This is one thing you'll see in all of his weekly RC emails.

599
00:53:58,950 --> 00:54:03,790
He will always give us sort of a feeling health warning of oh, there's a lot more patches in this than I expect.

600
00:54:03,790 --> 00:54:05,670
Like he does a lot of trend watching.

601
00:54:05,670 --> 00:54:10,790
So, he'll watch the trends in the last like five 10 kernels and he'll have a innate knowledge of this feels big.

602
00:54:10,790 --> 00:54:12,550
This feels like there's too many patches.

603
00:54:12,550 --> 00:54:15,030
Some of these fixes are big fixes.

604
00:54:15,030 --> 00:54:19,230
Like oh, he'll often look at the trend and say oh, there's like way too many patches here.

605
00:54:19,230 --> 00:54:21,630
But then he'll look at sampling of those patches and say oh, they're all quite small.

606
00:54:21,630 --> 00:54:23,350
They're all quite self-contained.

607
00:54:23,350 --> 00:54:24,870
They're fixing one small bug.

608
00:54:24,870 --> 00:54:26,630
It just happens we're fixing a lot of small bugs.

609
00:54:26,630 --> 00:54:34,390
But if he does that and he sees large changes like you're modifying multiple things in a subsystem that aren't reverts. Like we generally reverts are fine.

610
00:54:34,390 --> 00:54:38,150
But something that's doing a you know, a pretty intricate change to fix a bug.

611
00:54:38,150 --> 00:54:42,510
He will get a bit antsy and he'll be like, okay, no, I'm not too comfortable releasing RC7 straight to final.

612
00:54:42,510 --> 00:54:45,990
I might I think we should do an RC8. Quite often security issues can cause things like that.

613
00:54:45,990 --> 00:54:49,111
Like Spectre Meltdown, things like the big ones. Those those cause that problem.

614
00:54:49,120 --> 00:54:55,110
For me personally, yeah, I I keep an eye on sizing as well when I cuz I generate my weekly request to Linus.

615
00:54:55,110 --> 00:54:56,990
I'll keep an eye on sizing.

616
00:54:56,990 --> 00:55:03,431
Generally, I also keep an eye on the each so every week I generally get maybe four standard pull requests. I get our miscellaneous tree.

617
00:55:03,440 --> 00:55:07,590
I get the AMD folks, the Intel and two trees from different Intel teams.

618
00:55:07,590 --> 00:55:09,270
And those are like my baseline.

619
00:55:09,270 --> 00:55:11,830
If those don't come in by Friday, I'm like something's gone wrong.

620
00:55:11,830 --> 00:55:13,830
But then there'll always be maybe one other.

621
00:55:13,830 --> 00:55:18,790
Like so maybe MSM will come in with like the Qualcomm fixes for the for the They don't do weekly.

622
00:55:18,790 --> 00:55:21,390
So, like they might be two or three weeks I'll get a big bunch of those.

623
00:55:21,390 --> 00:55:25,910
And sometimes those ones are like, okay, there's a lot of changes in here. Could he have broken this up or is there some other things?

624
00:55:25,910 --> 00:55:27,950
And that's sort of like I I go down that gauge that.

625
00:55:27,950 --> 00:55:32,150
But for me, yeah, generally I find by RC4 or five, things do taper off.

626
00:55:32,150 --> 00:55:38,990
We do generally get a sort of a like a shorter things drop down to like the whole pull request being in less than 100 lines or 200 lines.

627
00:55:38,990 --> 00:55:45,070
If I start seeing pull requests where I'm seeing five or 600 lines changed that after RC56 I'm I will dig in a bit and look into it.

628
00:55:45,070 --> 00:55:48,750
But yeah, it's kind of a instinct built over time that you just your comfort levels.

629
00:55:48,750 --> 00:55:58,630
We usually find the really big problems early and it's then comes out to with graphics there's so many like yeah, if you break something central it's very obvious, but laptops are so diverse.

630
00:55:58,640 --> 00:56:12,270
There's so many different ways laptops can break and panels like it's not even one laptop it's like every model of a laptop could have different panel and the panel yeah, so it's like those things are always going to be on a bit more of a long tail, but they're not showstoppers.

631
00:56:12,270 --> 00:56:18,790
Like I generally treat a regression where somebody has a black screen as kind of a showstopper because it's like yeah, having a black screen means I can't install Linux.

632
00:56:18,790 --> 00:56:27,870
Yeah, I generally try to get those fixed as quickly as possible or by reverting or moving up you know, pushing people, but yeah, it's quite a hard to describe a showstopper when you're in the area of like yeah,

633
00:56:27,870 --> 00:56:31,990
every mostly works and the price of kernel as a whole the kernel even at RC1 mostly works pretty well.

634
00:56:32,000 --> 00:56:43,510
It's very rarely even at RC1 that it's a complete like what we call a dumpster fire, but the potential is still there, but the process is built in a quite well now that that's a lot rarer than it used to be.

635
00:56:43,510 --> 00:56:46,684
Like it used to be installing RC1 on your laptop was like will I have a file system tomorrow, you know.

636
00:56:46,694 --> 00:56:47,150
[laughter]

637
00:56:47,160 --> 00:56:54,110
Okay, that's really helpful and I think it gives us a good intuitive sense of what we mean by the health of an RC.

638
00:56:54,110 --> 00:57:01,910
In a moment I want to talk a little bit about how AI is changing your landscape and I also want to talk briefly about Rust in the Linux kernel.

639
00:57:01,910 --> 00:57:09,550
But before we move to that phase of our show and then draw the episode to a conclusion, is there anything that we haven't covered that you thought we should cover?

640
00:57:09,560 --> 00:57:12,270
No, I think we've gotten sort of the basic level topics.

641
00:57:12,280 --> 00:57:14,430
Okay.

642
00:57:14,430 --> 00:57:24,750
And if listeners are curious in wanting to know more, I'll pass along some links in the show notes both to other episodes of Software Engineering Radio and in many of the resources that Dave and I have gathered.

643
00:57:24,750 --> 00:57:30,670
Now quickly Dave, I wanted to talk about using AI because you've mentioned that several times.

644
00:57:30,670 --> 00:57:40,550
You talked about how you could have a coding agent do a review of part of the Linux kernel for you, and then maybe point out a potential regression or a security concern.

645
00:57:40,550 --> 00:57:44,870
Can you tell our listeners a little bit more about how you're integrating AI into your maintenance workflow?

646
00:57:44,880 --> 00:57:53,190
Well, over the last sort of year, this has become a like a topic of both interest and contention, I suppose, in the community.

647
00:57:53,190 --> 00:57:57,750
But, the way I personally got involved was at the last maintainer summit,

648
00:57:57,750 --> 00:58:08,990
Linus said like, "Oh, I'm actually seeing good reviews coming out of some of the like he he had had access to some people at Google and some people at Meta who had been doing some of this work, and they've been showing him some of the reviews, and he was like, "I'm quite impressed."

649
00:58:08,990 --> 00:58:14,710
And he says, "One thing that happens every year at maintainer summit is we complain about not having enough reviewer bandwidth, not having enough reviewers."

650
00:58:14,710 --> 00:58:16,870
You know, it's like it's a common refrain.

651
00:58:16,870 --> 00:58:20,710
And he's like, "If this can help or find problems, that's a good place.

652
00:58:20,710 --> 00:58:23,710
You know, we we should probably start looking into this."

653
00:58:23,710 --> 00:58:25,430
I was like, "Okay, well, that's a pretty good that he's he's interested."

654
00:58:25,440 --> 00:58:29,070
I talked to I called Chris Mason at Meta who's been leading their work on this.

655
00:58:29,080 --> 00:58:34,150
He actually wrote framework for regression finding for using AI.

656
00:58:34,150 --> 00:58:39,550
So, it was very specifically focused on prompting the AI to sort of find regressions.

657
00:58:39,550 --> 00:58:44,790
And how he tested it was by running it on older kernel patches, and then finding fixes for those older kernel patches.

658
00:58:44,790 --> 00:58:53,030
And if those fixes were for a regret And if the AI identified the initial problem in the initial patch review, then, you know, obviously the system is useful.

659
00:58:53,030 --> 00:58:55,110
And he's found that it was catching regressions.

660
00:58:55,110 --> 00:58:58,110
I I can't remember what the false positive was, but it's in the 50%.

661
00:58:58,110 --> 00:59:05,350
So, that's interesting, because if we can avoid having to put fixes patches in by having an automated reviewer and give you feedback, then why wouldn't we?

662
00:59:05,350 --> 00:59:11,230
Even if there's noise in that system, it's still better like the signal is on 50% is a pretty good signal noise.

663
00:59:11,230 --> 00:59:15,591
Even if it's not that, finding those regressions is still a lot of work. It's expensive.

664
00:59:15,600 --> 00:59:24,710
Like, when a regression actually gets into the Linux kernel and gets out into the world, that costs like actual money in the world, even if you're the process isn't seeing that.

665
00:59:24,710 --> 00:59:27,991
And having an AI find those, yeah, that there should be value in that. We need to work on it.

666
00:59:27,991 --> 00:59:32,910
What I personally did and this is due to a like a we also have a lot of AI stuff dealing with in Red Hat.

667
00:59:32,910 --> 00:59:37,910
So, I was like, well, I I was like, I'll take that mandate I have from Red Hat and Linus and sort of put together.

668
00:59:37,910 --> 00:59:49,230
So, I wrote a I used Claude to write a Claude reviewer where it just passes the patch series to Claude Opus 4 6 and then gives a good like patch by patch review. I didn't concentrate on regressions as much.

669
00:59:49,230 --> 00:59:54,350
I just wanted more of a an overall review of the series and a patch by patch review.

670
00:59:54,350 --> 00:59:56,710
Gives a basic set of problems that I haven't enforced this.

671
00:59:56,710 --> 00:59:58,590
I haven't pushed this onto the community.

672
00:59:58,590 --> 01:00:02,590
I built side public inbox infrastructure that was there on free desktop.

673
01:00:02,590 --> 01:00:09,150
So, you could actually use the same tools to pull this the patch reviews as you could pull patches with using the an alternate server.

674
01:00:09,150 --> 01:00:10,830
All the training should still work.

675
01:00:10,830 --> 01:00:12,430
So, it's there as an option right now.

676
01:00:12,430 --> 01:00:14,110
I'm also kind of using it as a reference.

677
01:00:14,110 --> 01:00:23,630
I'm I'm running it manually at the moment and generating it every day or two, but it's like if I see regressions and then I can go back and look at this reference and say, "Look at Look, it spotted that problem and now we have this problem months later."

678
01:00:23,630 --> 01:00:28,590
I'll get that feeling that maybe we should be pushing on this more.

679
01:00:28,590 --> 01:00:34,430
But, even last week or two Google unveiled I'm going to say it Shashlik or something I think it's called a patch review system.

680
01:00:34,430 --> 01:00:44,710
So, I need to catch up on that and maybe get them to start reviewing the DRM patches using that system if they're interested because my one is a bit junky, but it it was doing the job for me for what I want to figure out.

681
01:00:44,710 --> 01:00:48,350
But, I think the value at the moment people are definitely seeing value in AI patch review.

682
01:00:48,350 --> 01:00:55,070
Code generation, I still there's potential and I don't think I've seen a few patches generated with the help of Claude and they haven't been horrible.

683
01:00:55,080 --> 01:00:59,110
So, again, I still I'm quite open to see where that goes.

684
01:00:59,110 --> 01:01:04,110
I definitely think there will there will be potential for doing it and will pick up I I would guess in the next year.

685
01:01:04,110 --> 01:01:08,830
I think Opus and Gemini are starting to get to the level where you you can actually do some actually decent stuff with them.

686
01:01:08,830 --> 01:01:11,110
So, I think it will develop heavily in the next year.

687
01:01:11,120 --> 01:01:22,710
I know that we could do an entire episode just about using AI to do code review in the Linux kernel and I hope you'll continue to write blog posts on this topic because I'm sure our listeners will be excited to learn more.

688
01:01:22,720 --> 01:01:32,910
Before we end the show, I did want to talk about one other thing that I remember reading on your blog, which is the integration of the Rust programming language in the Linux kernel.

689
01:01:32,910 --> 01:01:40,990
Once more, I know that we could do a full episode on this topic, but can you give your our listeners a little sense of how Rust is playing a role in the Linux kernel?

690
01:01:41,000 --> 01:01:47,710
Yeah, actually my reasoning for using getting behind the Rust effort is actually not probably what people would expect.

691
01:01:47,710 --> 01:01:53,790
And I think you could probably do a really good episode with two people I know about Rust in the Linux kernel from a technical standpoint.

692
01:01:53,790 --> 01:01:59,830
My push for getting behind the Rust effort was actually more social and community focused.

693
01:01:59,830 --> 01:02:03,030
I was at I think it was Linux Plumbers Conference maybe 2 years, 3 years ago.

694
01:02:03,030 --> 01:02:04,590
Can't remember the exact timelines.

695
01:02:04,590 --> 01:02:11,470
So, maintainers summit the Linux 30 people summit that's co-located usually with Plumbers Conference and we have our private meeting at the pub conference.

696
01:02:11,470 --> 01:02:14,950
One of the big complaints in the private meeting over the years is we're all getting older.

697
01:02:14,950 --> 01:02:16,950
There's you know, we've got a lot more gray beards.

698
01:02:16,960 --> 01:02:21,510
Like people are in their 50s now that we're in their 20s when they started this journey.

699
01:02:21,510 --> 01:02:24,270
So, it's definitely the community's aging.

700
01:02:24,270 --> 01:02:29,790
And simultaneously at people complaining at that conference, I met some people from the graphics subsystem.

701
01:02:29,790 --> 01:02:31,630
Just starting to talk to people in the hallway tracks.

702
01:02:31,630 --> 01:02:36,030
And one guy came up to me and said, "Oh, I really like working in the graphics subsystem.

703
01:02:36,030 --> 01:02:37,910
There's loads of There's a young group of people working here.

704
01:02:37,920 --> 01:02:39,510
They're all very interesting, excited.

705
01:02:39,510 --> 01:02:42,390
I like going to conference and meeting them."

706
01:02:42,390 --> 01:02:48,310
And I I didn't know this existed really because I don't work down at the level, but she said there's this good community that she likes to be a part of.

707
01:02:48,310 --> 01:02:57,150
And I was like, "Okay, I didn't create that, but I we facilitated that in the DRM subsystem by being open to different things and not being so strict in our methods of developing the kernel."

708
01:02:57,150 --> 01:03:01,750
But then as well, the next time I was talking to some few of the Rust people and I was like, "These are very young people.

709
01:03:01,760 --> 01:03:06,550
These are again a group of people who are in their like maybe in their 20s, some in their 30s."

710
01:03:06,550 --> 01:03:11,750
Yeah, but they're they're a younger cohort of developers than the people I'm normally used to dealing with.

711
01:03:11,750 --> 01:03:15,870
And I was like, "Look, I think there is a good way we can bring these together.

712
01:03:15,870 --> 01:03:24,030
I think having young people coming into the kernel using Rust as a mechanism is is valuable and having it that different perspective and having it different way of doing it.

713
01:03:24,030 --> 01:03:35,711
And so I I was like, well, if I'm getting feedback that there's young people in the DRM sub system and it's an interesting area that to develop that in and there's young people writing well, I think I should be supportive of putting Rust in the kernel. I see it going to happen.

714
01:03:35,711 --> 01:03:37,511
I'll help accelerate it. I'll facilitate it.

715
01:03:37,520 --> 01:03:41,590
And I I I I stood up at the Rust conference mini conference and said, "Yeah, I'm open to doing this.

716
01:03:41,590 --> 01:03:43,790
Let's do it."

717
01:03:43,790 --> 01:03:47,950
Now, in terms of when I say that of me doing anything about it I didn't do anything about it.

718
01:03:47,950 --> 01:03:50,870
I just said I was happy to have people do something about it.

719
01:03:50,870 --> 01:03:54,310
And that's been my So it's my working model over the years is like just say you're open to the idea.

720
01:03:54,310 --> 01:03:56,350
Don't You don't have to do it.

721
01:03:56,350 --> 01:03:59,750
It's like I'm willing to make space for this to happen and and facilitate it.

722
01:03:59,750 --> 01:04:12,350
And what I actually end up doing was going back inside Red Hat, finding one of my engineers who was a a working on the stuff in New Folsom at the time and he was a really good C developer and suggesting to them like they don't work for me.

723
01:04:12,350 --> 01:04:14,350
They're in my one of my teams, but I I'm not a manager.

724
01:04:14,350 --> 01:04:16,110
I was like, "Would you be interested in Rust?"

725
01:04:16,110 --> 01:04:20,511
And they went through the cycle of I don't really like this. I don't really like it. And now they really love it.

726
01:04:20,511 --> 01:04:24,750
And now they're one of the Rust core maintainers. So it's like encouraging, building that group.

727
01:04:24,750 --> 01:04:33,190
Like you know, if you wanted to talk about the technical aspects of Rust in the kernel, talk to someone like Danilo or Miguel would be ideal because they really know the details of it.

728
01:04:33,190 --> 01:04:38,230
For me, it is just about bringing in new people into the community and expanding the Linux kernel community.

729
01:04:38,230 --> 01:04:40,070
And like I see the safety aspects and I see those aspects of it.

730
01:04:40,070 --> 01:04:46,030
I I I actually see good value in them, especially we have a graphics sub system.

731
01:04:46,030 --> 01:04:49,311
We have a very wide API to the user space. It's often exploited.

732
01:04:49,311 --> 01:04:51,190
So I would rather we don't have that.

733
01:04:51,190 --> 01:04:54,070
I'd rather that's a safe API as much as possible.

734
01:04:54,070 --> 01:04:59,310
And same with the hardware that we keep that safety concerns with the hardware interfaces as much as possible.

735
01:04:59,310 --> 01:05:01,790
I see Rust accelerating the ability to deliver GPU drivers.

736
01:05:01,790 --> 01:05:04,990
I see it being much faster that we can build a lot more common infrastructure.

737
01:05:04,990 --> 01:05:08,550
We can avoid making a lot of the C mistakes we've repeatedly make.

738
01:05:08,550 --> 01:05:13,150
You know, a long time C developers will say, "We could do that in C."

739
01:05:13,150 --> 01:05:15,230
And I'm like, yes, we can, but we've repeatedly not.

740
01:05:15,230 --> 01:05:19,230
You know, yeah, review can catch this, but we've repeatedly failed.

741
01:05:19,230 --> 01:05:26,790
It's like, yes, like these are all the fact that yes, if you have the best C program in the world on a bad day, we'll still make that mistake.

742
01:05:26,790 --> 01:05:28,351
Rust gives you a lot more you can't do that. The compiler stops you.

743
01:05:28,360 --> 01:05:36,030
You can build into your type system the fact that the compiler will stop you doing you know, let you do a stupid things and especially with lifetimes.

744
01:05:36,040 --> 01:05:39,310
Lifetimes are one of the hardest things to get right in the Linux kernel.

745
01:05:39,310 --> 01:05:44,230
The lifetime we made this like 20 years of lifetime mistakes that would describe a lot of the kernel development process.

746
01:05:44,240 --> 01:05:47,670
Like it we have made every lifetime mistake over the years, you know.

747
01:05:47,670 --> 01:05:59,150
In terms of, you know, from when we moved from single processor to SMP, but even moving now to threaded like interrupts and all these things all these changes over the years find new ways that we've messed up our lifetimes.

748
01:05:59,150 --> 01:06:01,270
Hot plugging devices, lifetimes messed up.

749
01:06:01,270 --> 01:06:08,310
They've all involved been designing new things and you know, getting that right and embedding that in your type system is quite valuable to stop making those mistakes.

750
01:06:08,320 --> 01:06:09,910
Yeah, thanks for all of these insights.

751
01:06:09,920 --> 01:06:23,870
It's really been a lot of fun to have this conversation as we've been focusing on Linux kernel maintenance and I recognize there's many technical details that we didn't really cover, but I'm now interested in you taking a big step back as we conclude our episode.

752
01:06:23,870 --> 01:06:27,670
So, you've been working on kernel maintenance for more than two decades.

753
01:06:27,670 --> 01:06:32,710
Can you tell us a little bit briefly what has stayed the same and what has changed?

754
01:06:32,710 --> 01:06:36,070
Give our listeners a little bit of a picture as we conclude the episode.

755
01:06:36,080 --> 01:06:41,830
Yeah, it's an interesting question. I think the essentials of it have stayed the same.

756
01:06:41,830 --> 01:06:46,430
I think the basic you write a patch, you fix a problem, goes through the tree.

757
01:06:46,430 --> 01:06:47,950
That that that has the core of it has remained the same.

758
01:06:47,950 --> 01:06:57,430
There's like, yeah, there's a mailing list, there was people, but the scale of it has so much changed and the level for yes, you have a patch to it has gone from like, you know,

759
01:06:57,430 --> 01:07:03,870
yeah, you have a a patch, but now you should probably a lot more you need to do more with that patch to get it to a quality standard that's acceptable for the kernel.

760
01:07:03,870 --> 01:07:07,630
I think the quality of the kernel has changed substantially in that 20 years.

761
01:07:07,630 --> 01:07:17,110
The quality of our process for producing it has solidified into an actual process as opposed to what it was 10 20 years ago where it was just like randomly just you know, there was no timelines, there was no process.

762
01:07:17,110 --> 01:07:19,310
It was very shoot from the hip, I suppose.

763
01:07:19,310 --> 01:07:23,150
So, now it's like yeah, it's very formalized, it's very structured.

764
01:07:23,150 --> 01:07:33,790
I would say it's like going to be less fun, but less fun is important in that like yeah, you can't make suddenly wide raging memory management changes that destabilizes half the world's computers.

765
01:07:33,800 --> 01:07:36,790
That's probably not a good thing to be doing anymore.

766
01:07:36,790 --> 01:07:44,070
So, yeah, it's like you know, you definitely have it's matured a lot, but I still think the core aspect of hacking on hardware devices and stuff is still at least for me very appealing.

767
01:07:44,080 --> 01:07:54,110
Like I still yesterday I was writing code for Nvidia GPU drivers even though it's you know, it's just stuff to do and it's like I still like having a hardware device.

768
01:07:54,110 --> 01:08:01,630
I just got an Nvidia Spark box and I just getting that lit up with open source Linux drivers is like something that still appeals to me even 20 years later like that whole aspect of it.

769
01:08:01,640 --> 01:08:05,191
So, thank you very much for your enthusiasm. It's really contagious.

770
01:08:05,191 --> 01:08:10,750
And if I could say on a personal note, for someone who's been using Linux for decades myself,

771
01:08:10,750 --> 01:08:21,710
thank you for all of the work you and your many colleagues have done because for me using Linux has been a genuine source of joy in the same way that this has been a joy-filled and awesome conversation.

772
01:08:21,710 --> 01:08:25,030
Dave, is there anything that we haven't covered that you think we should conclude on?

773
01:08:25,040 --> 01:08:32,190
No, I think just people if you're interested in the Linux kernel, just get out there and find a niche, scratch it, submit some patches, you know, the community's out there.

774
01:08:32,190 --> 01:08:34,150
We're we're always happy to have new people join it.

775
01:08:34,160 --> 01:08:35,870
Okay.

776
01:08:35,870 --> 01:08:38,269
Thanks for all these insights, Dave.

